        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "d2jddxc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Crust"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The interior of the earth can be divided into three major layers: crust, mantle, and core.",
      "id": "ie8fhme"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g4lze6x",
      "content": "• The crust is the outermost layer of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5qke5dd",
      "content": "• It is very thin compared to other layers."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "65hjfy5",
      "content": "• Average thickness is about 35 km under continents."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "78lod85",
      "content": "• Oceanic crust is thinner (5–10 km)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p2chkem",
      "content": "• Main elements are oxygen, silicon, aluminum, iron and calcium."
    },
    {
      "id": "gkp8deh",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Crust"
    },
    {
      "id": "xymas2w",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Thickness</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Composition</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Continental Crust</p>",
            "<p>30–70 km</p>",
            "<p>Granite rocks rich in silica and alumina</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Oceanic Crust</p>",
            "<p>5–10 km</p>",
            "<p>Basalt rocks rich in silica and magnesium</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "73acwj5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ixbfyew",
      "content": "• The mantle lies below the crust and extends up to about 2900 km depth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dvy8k5r",
      "content": "• It forms nearly 84% of Earth's volume."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Continental Crust (SIAL)",
        "Oceanic Crust (SIMA)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Primary Composition",
          "Silica and Aluminium",
          "Silica and Magnesium"
        ],
        [
          "Average Thickness",
          "Thicker (35 - 70 km)",
          "Thinner (5 - 10 km)"
        ],
        [
          "Average Density",
          "Lower (~2.7 g/cm³)",
          "Higher (~3.0 g/cm³)"
        ],
        [
          "Dominant Rock Type",
          "Granite / Felsic Rocks",
          "Basalt / Mafic Rocks"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "dbbne70"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d2dxx7x",
      "content": "• The upper part of the mantle with the crust forms the lithosphere."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wr5ydy0",
      "content": "• Below it lies the asthenosphere which contains partially molten rocks."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p31t7w6",
      "content": "• The mantle plays a major role in plate tectonic movements."
    },
    {
      "id": "mmfe2fc",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Important Zones in the Mantle"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "drvyqrl",
      "content": "• Lithosphere – rigid outer layer including crust and upper mantle."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h6982hc",
      "content": "• Asthenosphere – semi-molten layer where tectonic plates float."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mkf3olf",
      "content": "• Mesosphere – lower mantle with higher density."
    },
    {
      "id": "9qiihbj",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5zbziqr",
      "content": "• The core is the innermost part of the Earth."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qr8gr2t",
      "content": "• It extends from 2900 km to the center at 6371 km."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pdsaj5i",
      "content": "• It is mainly composed of iron and nickel."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fom3la9",
      "content": "• The core is responsible for generating Earth's magnetic field."
    },
    {
      "id": "chq2vlu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Types of Core"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Mohorovičić Discontinuity (Moho) lies between the Crust and the Upper Mantle.",
        "Gutenberg Discontinuity lies between the Lower Mantle and the Outer Core at a depth of 2,900 km.",
        "S-waves cannot pass through the liquid Outer Core, creating a shadow zone between 103° and 142° from the epicenter.",
        "Asthenosphere is a semi-fluid layer in the upper mantle responsible for plate tectonic movement."
      ],
      "id": "jv4z78w"
    },
